# Phase 5 — First-class constraints

Add:

```text
constraint("gt", $x, 10)
```

as an ordinary expression.

Extend:

```text
Value
```

with:

```text
Constraint
```

and define:

```text
Constraint
  name: String
  arguments: List<ConstraintExpr>
```

This is the most important semantic decision because it means you don't need
special semantics for constraints in `map`, `filter`, bindings, etc.

The current language is expression-oriented and documents are already ordinary
values; this extension follows that same philosophy. 

---

# Phase 6 — Constraint expression normalization

Do **not** reuse arbitrary `Expr` as the stored representation of a
constraint argument.

Introduce:

```text
ConstraintExpr
  = Ref
      path: Path

  | Literal
      value: JSON

  | Array
      elements: List<ConstraintExpr>

  | Object
      fields: List<(String, ConstraintExpr)>

  | Field
      target: ConstraintExpr
      name: String
```

The evaluator converts the ordinary expression supplied to `constraint(...)`
into this restricted representation.

For example:

```tramaj
constraint(
  "gt",
  $my-unknown,
  $ctx.threshold
)
```

becomes conceptually:

```text
Constraint(
  "gt",
  [
    Ref(["my-unknown"]),
    Ref(["ctx", "threshold"])
  ]
)
```

### Validity rules

Initially allow:

* [ ] references;
* [ ] static field access;
* [ ] `null`;
* [ ] booleans;
* [ ] numbers;
* [ ] strings;
* [ ] arrays;
* [ ] objects.

Reject:

* [ ] arbitrary function calls;
* [ ] lambdas;
* [ ] imports;
* [ ] `lookup`;
* [ ] document values;
* [ ] actions;
* [ ] `map` nested inside a constraint argument.

The important distinction is:

```tramaj
map($items, (x) => constraint(...))
```

**is valid**.

But:

```tramaj
constraint("foo", map(...))
```

is not necessarily valid.

The former creates constraints as ordinary values. The latter attempts to put
an arbitrary computation *inside the declarative constraint representation*.

---

# Phase 7 — Context values inside constraints

Explicitly test:

```tramaj
@x : Int = ?

constraint("gt", $x, $ctx.threshold)
```

and:

```tramaj
constraint(
  "equal",
  $x.foo,
  $ctx.configuration.foo
)
```

The important implementation point is:

> A constraint argument reference is not necessarily symbolic.

It may resolve to:

```text
Unknown
```

or:

```text
Number
String
Boolean
Object
Array
...
```

The constraint retains the reference rather than eagerly requiring it to be
symbolic.

This gives you the desired concrete/symbolic mixture.

---

# Phase 8 — Make `map` constraint-aware automatically

This should hopefully require **almost no evaluator change**.

Given:

```tramaj
map(
  $items,
  (x) => constraint("gt", $x, 0)
)
```

the existing `Map` evaluator should simply obtain:

```text
Array<Value>
```

where each `Value` happens to be a `Constraint`.

So:

* [ ] Add `Constraint` to the value type.
* [ ] Verify existing `map` implementation is polymorphic over result values.
* [ ] Add tests for `map` returning constraints.
* [ ] Add tests where mapped items are concrete.
* [ ] Add tests where mapped items are symbolic.
* [ ] Add tests with mixed concrete/symbolic items.

Ideally there should be **zero `if result is Constraint` logic inside `map`**.

That is a good architectural test.

---

# Phase 9 — Constraint collection

Don't make constraint construction a side effect.

For example:

```tramaj
@constraints =
  map(
    $items,
    (x) => constraint("valid", $x)
  )
```

simply produces an array.

Then define an analyzer-side operation:

```text
collectConstraints(Value) -> List<Constraint>
```

which recursively traverses:

```text
Array
Object
```

and perhaps whatever program-level roots you decide are relevant.

### Important design question

Before implementing this, settle:

> **Where does an analyzer look for constraints?**

I would initially support explicit collection from values/bindings rather than
magically treating every constraint anywhere in the program as a global side
effect.

For example:

```tramaj
@constraints = [...]
```

is much easier to reason about than:

```text
every Constraint encountered during evaluation mutates global analyzer state
```

The latter would fight Tramaj's existing value semantics.

---

# Phase 10 — Symbolic field access

This is the first genuinely interesting evaluator change.

Suppose:

```tramaj
@item : $foo.types.Item = ?
```

and later:

```tramaj
$item.title
```

The evaluator cannot actually read `title` from a concrete object.

It needs to produce a symbolic projection.

I would therefore **not make `Unknown` contain a mutable object/domain**.

Instead introduce something like:

```text
Unknown
  identity: SymbolId
  type: Type

SymbolicPath
  root: SymbolId
  path: List<String>
```

or make symbolic values themselves path expressions.

For example:

```text
$item.title
```

could become:

```text
Unknown(
  identity = item,
  type = $foo.types.Item.title
)
```

But I'd prefer a representation closer to:

```text
SymbolicValue
  origin: SymbolId
  path: List<String>
  type: Type
```

because it will become very useful to the constraint analyzer.

Then:

```tramaj
constraint("gt", $item.price, 0)
```

doesn't require evaluating `$item.price`.

It produces a reference to the symbolic projection.

**This is the piece I would prototype before committing the final V2 AST.**

---

# Phase 11 — Symbolic values crossing Node boundaries

The current Node JSON spec says Node values are ordinary JSON values and explicitly
makes JSON the interchange boundary for attributes, element values and action
payloads. 

So don't quietly serialize:

```json
{"type": "unknown", ...}
```

as if that were already V2-compatible Node JSON.

First decide one of these two designs:

### A. Symbolic Node is a V2 extension of Node JSON

Extend the normative value JSON representation with a discriminator:

```json
{
  "type": "unknown",
  "id": "...",
  "valueType": "..."
}
```

### B. Keep Node JSON strictly concrete

Require a symbolic/constraint-aware host to consume a richer **V2 Value/Node
wire format**, while V1 Node JSON continues to mean concrete JSON.

I currently prefer **B**.

The current Node JSON document deliberately says `Value` is ordinary JSON and
that Nodes don't nest through attribute/value/action boundaries. 

Don't destabilize that boundary until we've designed symbolic serialization
properly.

---

# Phase 12 — Annotations

The existing annotations mechanism is already exactly the right escape hatch:

```text
Annotations = Map<String, JSON>
```

and the spec explicitly reserves it for types, domains, provenance and host
metadata. 

For V2:

* [ ] Preserve annotation semantics unchanged.
* [ ] Don't make core evaluation interpret arbitrary annotations.
* [ ] Define an analyzer namespace/key convention only if needed.
* [ ] Ensure all Node transformations preserve annotations.
* [ ] Add tests around `adapt-actions`.
* [ ] Don't use annotations as a replacement for the semantic `Unknown` value.

Annotations should carry **derived metadata**, not be the fundamental
representation of symbolic values.

---

# Phase 13 — Type checking of ordinary expressions

Once types exist, implement the minimum type inference needed by the analyzer:

```text
$x : T
$x.field : T.field
```

For products:

```text
Card.title : String
Card.author : Author
```

For arrays:

```text
Array<T>[...] : T
```

For known literals:

```text
3       : Int
"foo"   : String
true    : Bool
```

The precise built-in type names (`Int`, `String`, `Bool`, etc.) should be
settled before this stage.

Don't implement general type inference yet.

---

# Phase 14 — Constraint dictionary

Introduce an analyzer API along the lines of:

```text
ConstraintDefinition {
  name
  validate(arguments, typingEnvironment)
  analyze(arguments, state)
}
```

The Tramaj core should only know:

```text
Constraint(name, arguments)
```

The analyzer owns the meaning.

Start with tiny examples:

```text
gt
gte
lt
lte
eq
```

but don't hard-code them into the language evaluator.

This is particularly important because the existing language intentionally
keeps builtin vocabulary fixed while host-specific semantic vocabularies can
remain outside the core. 

---

# Phase 15 — First domain propagation

Once constraints exist as data, implement **one deliberately small solver**.

For example:

```tramaj
@x : Int = ?

constraint("gte", $x, 1)
constraint("lte", $x, 10)
```

should produce:

```text
x : Int
domain: 1..10
```

Start with:

```text
Int
 └── interval domain
```

only.

Support:

* [ ] lower bound;
* [ ] upper bound;
* [ ] intersection;
* [ ] contradiction;
* [ ] exact singleton.

Don't implement arithmetic expressions yet. The current language explicitly
has no arithmetic operators or arithmetic builtins. 

---

# Phase 16 — Concrete propagation

Then test:

```tramaj
@x : Int = ?

constraint("gt", $x, $ctx.threshold)
```

where:

```text
ctx.threshold = 10
```

should produce:

```text
x : Int
domain: 11..∞
```

And:

```tramaj
constraint("gt", $x, 10)
constraint("lt", $x, 5)
```

should report a contradiction.

This is where the distinction between:

```text
symbolic reference
```

and:

```text
concrete value
```

pays off.

---

# Phase 17 — Constraint propagation through `map`

Finally test the use case that motivated first-class constraints:

```tramaj
@constraints =
  map(
    $ctx.items,
    (item) => constraint(
      "gt",
      $item.replicas,
      $ctx.minimumReplicas
    )
  )
```

The analyzer should recursively collect the resulting constraints and
propagate them.

If the items are themselves symbolic:

```text
item[0].replicas : Int
item[1].replicas : Int
...
```

you should get independent symbolic variables/projections.

This is a very good integration test for the whole architecture.

---

# Phase 18 — Import/type integration

Test:

```tramaj
@lib = import("deployment", {})

@deployment : $lib.types.Deployment = ?
```

Then:

```tramaj
constraint(
  "valid-replicas",
  $deployment.replicas,
  $ctx.maxReplicas
)
```

The analyzer needs to resolve:

```text
$lib.types.Deployment
        │
        ▼
nominal type
        │
        ▼
Deployment.replicas
        │
        ▼
Int
```

No library evaluation should be required merely to resolve its types.

This should also work through nested imports.

